## Chapter 1 Introduction

The dynamics of both matter and energy in nature are best understood in terms of the kinematics and interactions of fundamental particles. A small core of fundamental laws and theories govern the behavior and interaction of all types of matter and energy. A major goal in physics is to find out the common ground that would unite all of these theories into one integrated theory of everything. The Standard Model of particle physics describes three of the four known fundamental interactions _viz._ the Electromagnetic, the Weak and the Strong interactions between the elementary particles. The Standard Model combines the Electroweak theory, a combined theory of Electromagnetic and Weak interactions, together with Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) which is the theory of strong interactions. Unfortunately, it has so far not been possible to unite Gravity into such a unified theory. The Standard Model of particle physics contains 12 flavors of elementary fermions (3 quarks, 3 leptons and their corresponding antiparticles) together with elementary bosons that mediate the forces. Figure 1.1 depicts the constituents of the Standard model showing the elementary particles and the mediators of the interactions.

